<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Zahir-ud-din Muhammad Babur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babur was a Timurid prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. After losing his homeland in Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, he established himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. In 1526 he defeated the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> at the Battle of Panipat, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and his memoir, the Baburnama, is a famous autobiographical work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" width="270" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: c.1650 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Babur was born on 14 February 1483 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andijan">Andijan</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>, the eldest son of Umar Shaikh Mirza II, ruler of Fergana, and of Qutlugh Nigar Khanum, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a>, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> and a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. He came from the Turkic Barlas tribe, which was of Mongol origin and had embraced the Turco-Persian tradition. His first language was Chagatai, and he was equally fluent in Classical Persian. The nickname Babur, from the Persian word for tiger or panther, may have been easier for his Turco-Mongol army to pronounce than Zahir ud-Din Muhammad.</p><p>His father died, by one account, in a dovecote that toppled into a ravine, and in 1494 Babur became the Timurid ruler of Fergana as a boy. Two uncles and nobles who preferred his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> threatened his succession, and he secured his throne mainly with help from his maternal grandmother. He lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> three times, the last to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1504 Babur conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. After losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> for the third time he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, reaching the Chenab River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1519. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> was crumbling, and Babur received invitations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-khan-lodi">Daulat Khan Lodi</a>, the governor of Punjab, and from Ibrahim's uncle Ala-ud-Din. In 1526 he defeated and killed Ibrahim at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. He then defeated a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and Afghan warlords under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> of Mewar at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a>, through skilful troop positioning and the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>.</p><p>After Panipat his main difficulty was holding the territory, since many Central Asian followers found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> hot and alien and wanted to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. He gave away the captured treasure generously. From Ottoman commanders he adopted the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> in the field, not only in sieges. According to the Baburnama, his campaign in northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> targeted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> as well as non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> sects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Babur died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>. He was first buried in Agra and, as he had wished, reburied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> between 1539 and 1544. He wrote the Baburnama, a memoir in Chagatai Turkic that was translated into Persian under his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. A passionate maker of gardens, he introduced the symmetrical four-part charbagh to the plains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where it became a trademark of Mughal architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last, Kalimullah, fled Bidar around 1527 and sought help from <b>Babur</b>, the new Mughal ruler of Delhi, and later from Bijapur and Ahmadnagar, dying in exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of the Mughals and Afghans in the north brought harder enemies: <b>Babur</b> defeated a Bengal army at the Battle of the Ghaghra in 1529, and Sher Khan Sur defeated the sultan's forces at Surajgarh in 1534 and twice besieged Gaur, taking it in 1538 despite Portuguese…</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Babur</b>, founder of the Mughal Empire in India (1526), was a Timurid on his father's side and a descendant of the Chagataid khans through his mother, so the Chagatai inheritance passed into one of the most consequential lines of descent in Asian imperial history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibrahim Lodi's much larger army, reportedly some 100,000 strong, was destroyed there by <b>Babur</b>'s combination of field guns and matchlocks behind a barrier of linked carts and the flanking tulughma manoeuvre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Babur</b> descended from Timur on his father's side and from Genghis Khan on his mother's, and his dynasty's name comes from the Persian word for Mongol.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad Shaybani Khan's campaigns of 1500–1507 drove the Timurid prince <b>Babur</b> from Samarkand and Transoxiana and extinguished the Herat branch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andijan">Andijan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.78,72.35&amp;year=1513" aria-label="Andijan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6322 707 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Babur"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6772 977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6772 977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.78,72.35&amp;year=1513">What was here in 1513? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/humayun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/humayun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Humayun</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1508–1556</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Few towns in the whole habitable world are so pleasant as Samarkand.&#8221;</p><footer>Bābur-nāma, on Samarkand &#183; tr. Annette S. Beveridge, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44608/pg44608-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I put my foot in the stirrup of resolution, set my hand on the rein of trust in God…&#8221;</p><footer>Bābur-nāma, setting out against Ibrāhīm Lodī, 1525 &#183; tr. Annette S. Beveridge, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44608/pg44608-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…a melon was brought to me; to cut and eat it affected me strangely; I was all tears!&#8221;</p><footer>Bābur-nāma, letter to Khwāja Kalān, 1529 &#183; tr. Annette S. Beveridge, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44608/pg44608-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I reflected, as I rode, that the wish to cease from sin had been always in my mind, and that my forbidden acts had set lasting stain upon my heart.&#8221;</p><footer>Bābur-nāma, on renouncing wine before the battle of Khanwa, 1527 &#183; tr. Annette S. Beveridge, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44608/pg44608-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sometimes like the madmen, I used to wander alone over hill and plain; sometimes I betook myself to gardens and the suburbs, lane by lane.&#8221;</p><footer>Bābur-nāma, on a youthful infatuation, 1500 &#183; tr. Annette S. Beveridge, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44608/pg44608-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if a forefather have done ill, his ill must be changed for good.&#8221;</p><footer>Bābur-nāma, on the customs of Chinggis Khan's law, 1507 &#183; tr. Annette S. Beveridge, 1922 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44608/pg44608-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
